> still a game? If Rodney steals the Amulet from me as I ascend, and 
> I can't find him so I escape instead of ascending, still a game?

In my opinion; it is a [played] game.

> Is a death where I fire a lightning bolt at myself still a game?

Yes, sure it is.

> Does it matter if I do it on turn 1 or turn 10 or turn 10,000?

Yes, I'd say this is a crucial criterion.

You can observe on NAO that there's many startscummed games that
precede by many quit [non-]"games"; the latter I wouldn't actually
consider to qualify as played games.

If we have a closer look at the numbers (data based on a logfile
from Mai 2014) we see for example...

  984965: all quit games
   60085:   subset of quit games with more than 1 turns

and a similar relation of played vs. non-played with escaped games.

I acknowledge that there are still escaped games that have only a
few turns played before leaving that would not really be valid as
"played". Though I don't think it would be necessary to define a
formally perfect metric as long as those cases contribute only to
less than 10% of the respective cases, and specifically not for
the informal entry page, which certainly serves only as sort of
an advertisment for NAO. But obvious started but non-played games
should IMO be excluded.[*]

> 
> Perhaps we can skip drawing lines in the sand about what a game is
> and what it isn't, and instead, show histograms of game length in
> turns, by outcome.

The entry webpage shows only a number, not detailled information.
For my taste it would be sufficient to say "More than 1.1 Million
played games.", and that would certainly be a more significant
number than the "3,146,839" we currently see, which is neither
justified in the quantity nor in the quality that is implicitly
suggested by the many digits of "accuracy".[**]

Janis

